\begin{abstract}
For an integer $t \ge 2$ let $N(t)$ denote the number of pairs $(n,k)$ with $0 \le k \le n$
and $\binom{n}{k} = t$; this is the multiplicity of $t$ in Pascal's triangle. Singmaster
conjectured in 1971 that $N$ is bounded on $\{t : t \ge 2\}$; the conjecture remains open.
We develop an elementary but complete structure theory for $N$: finiteness with the explicit
localisation $n \le t$; a strict-monotonicity pair implying that each column carries a value
at most once and each row at most twice; the unconditional bound $N(t) \le 2\log_2 t$; the
exact values $N(2)=1$, $N(3)=N(4)=N(5)=2$, $N(6)=3$, $N(10)=4$ and $N(p)=2$ for every odd
prime $p$; and the reduction $N(t) \le 2\lvert R(t)\rvert$, where $R(t)$ is the set of rows
meeting $t$, which makes Singmaster's conjecture equivalent to a statement about rows alone.

The central structural result is a \emph{parity criterion}: $N(t)$ is odd if and only if $t$
is a central binomial coefficient $\binom{2m}{m}$. Since the empirical mystery of Singmaster's
problem is the total absence of numbers with $N(t)\in\{5,7\}$, this reduces that question to a
single density-zero sequence. We then make the reduction effective. A sandwich theorem shows
that $\binom{2m}{m}$ strictly dominates every other entry in the triangle truncated at row
$2m$; a column collapse and a triangular obstruction shrink the resulting search box for
$m=20$ from roughly $524248 \times 262124$ to $9347 \times 17$. Executing the searches gives
$N\!\left(\binom{2m}{m}\right)=3$ for all $2 \le m \le 20$ and hence, unconditionally, no
$t < 538{,}257{,}874{,}440$ satisfies $N(t)\in\{5,7\}$. A certified decision procedure over a
box of side $O(\sqrt t)$ yields $N(3003)=8$, $N(120)=N(210)=N(1540)=N(7140)=N(11628)=N(24310)=6$,
and the statement that the maximum of $N$ below $10^6$ is $8$, attained only at $t=3003$.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The problem}

Pascal's triangle is the array of binomial coefficients $\binom{n}{k}$, $0 \le k \le n$,
generated by $\binom{n}{0}=\binom{n}{n}=1$ and Pascal's rule
$\binom{n+1}{k+1} = \binom{n}{k}+\binom{n}{k+1}$. For $t \ge 2$ define
\[
N(t) := \#\left\{(n,k)\in\mathbb{N}^2 : k \le n,\ \binom{n}{k}=t\right\}.
\]
The value $1$ is excluded because it occurs infinitely often along both edges. Every other
value occurs finitely often (Proposition~\ref{prop:loc}), and the table of multiplicities is
remarkable for its poverty:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
$t$ & $2$ & $3,4,5$ & $6$ & $10$ & $120,210,1540,7140,11628,24310$ & $3003$\\
\midrule
$N(t)$ & $1$ & $2$ & $3$ & $4$ & $6$ & $8$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Multiplicity $2$ is generic; multiplicities $1,3,4,6,8$ occur; multiplicities $5$ and $7$ have
never been observed; and no multiplicity exceeding $8$ has ever been observed. Singmaster
(1971) conjectured that $N$ is bounded; a strong form asserts $\max_{t\ge2}N(t)=8$, attained
only at $t=3003$.

\subsection{Outline}

Section~\ref{sec:elem} sets up the elementary theory. Section~\ref{sec:log} derives the
unconditional bound $N(t)\le 2\log_2 t$ and the row reformulation. Section~\ref{sec:small}
treats small values and primes. Section~\ref{sec:fib} explains the abundance of sixes.
Section~\ref{sec:parity} proves the parity criterion. Section~\ref{sec:eff} makes the
reduction effective. Section~\ref{sec:exact} gives the exact decision procedure and the
maximality of $3003$ below $10^6$. Section~\ref{sec:disc} discusses limitations and open
problems.

\section{Elementary structure theory}\label{sec:elem}

Throughout, $n,k,t,m$ denote non-negative integers and $\binom{n}{k}=0$ for $k>n$.

\subsection{Monotonicity down a column}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:step}
If $1 \le k \le n$ then $\binom{n}{k}<\binom{n+1}{k}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Write $k=j+1$. Pascal's rule gives $\binom{n+1}{j+1}=\binom{n}{j}+\binom{n}{j+1}$ and
$\binom{n}{j}>0$ since $j \le n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:mono}
If $1 \le k \le n < n'$ then $\binom{n}{k}<\binom{n'}{k}$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $n'$ using Lemma~\ref{lem:step}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Column uniqueness]\label{cor:coluniq}
For fixed $k \ge 1$, a value occurs at most once in column $k$.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Localisation and finiteness}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:rowle}
If $1 \le k \le n-1$ then $n \le \binom{n}{k}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Strong induction on $n$. For $k=1$ the entry is $n$. If $2 \le k$ and $k+1<n$, write $n=p+1$,
$k=j+1$; then $\binom{p+1}{j+1}=\binom{p}{j}+\binom{p}{j+1}\ge p+p \ge p+1$. If $k=n-1$,
symmetry reduces to $k=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Localisation]\label{prop:loc}
If $\binom{n}{k}=t \ge 2$ with $k \le n$, then $1 \le k \le n-1$ and $n \le t$.
\end{proposition}

Consequently the occurrence set $\Occ(t):=\{(n,k): k\le n,\ \binom{n}{k}=t\}$ lies in the
finite box $[0,t]^2$, so $N(t)=\lvert\Occ(t)\rvert$ is a well-defined computable number.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:choose2}
If $2 \le k \le n-2$ then $\binom{n}{2}\le\binom{n}{k}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Strong induction on $n$ using Pascal's rule for both sides together with
Lemma~\ref{lem:rowle}; the case $k=n-2$ follows by symmetry.
\end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:choose2} is the workhorse of all row-window bounds: an interior occurrence of
$t$ forces $\binom{n}{2}\le t$, i.e.\ $n = O(\sqrt t)$.

\subsection{Geometric growth in the column index}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:central2k}
$2^k \le \binom{2k}{k}$ for all $k$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induction: $\binom{2k+2}{k+1}=2\binom{2k+1}{k+1}\ge 2\binom{2k}{k}$, using the symmetry of
row $2k+1$ and Pascal's rule.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:growth}
If $2k \le n$ then $2^k \le \binom{n}{k}$.
\end{proposition}

\subsection{Strict unimodality of a row}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:unistep}
If $2(k+1)\le n$ then $\binom{n}{k}<\binom{n}{k+1}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
From $\binom{n}{k+1}(k+1)=\binom{n}{k}(n-k)$ and $n-k>k+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Strict unimodality]\label{prop:uni}
If $j<j'$ and $2j' \le n$ then $\binom{n}{j}<\binom{n}{j'}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{corollary}[At most two per row]\label{cor:tworow}
For any $n$ and $t$, $\#\{k \le n: \binom{n}{k}=t\}\le 2$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The folding map $k \mapsto \min(k,n-k)$ preserves the value and is at most $2$-to-$1$;
Proposition~\ref{prop:uni} makes it injective on the folded range.
\end{proof}

\section{The unconditional logarithmic bound}\label{sec:log}

For a position $p=(n,k)$ set $\fold(p):=\min(k,n-k)$. Then
$\binom{n}{\fold(p)}=\binom{n}{k}$ and $2\fold(p)\le n$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:bdry}
If $t \ge 2$, $\binom{n}{k}=t$ and $\fold(n,k)\le 1$, then $(n,k)\in\{(t,1),(t,t-1)\}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:foldlog}
If $\binom{n}{k}=t\ge 2$, then $\fold(n,k)\le\log_2 t$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
With $j=\fold(n,k)$ we have $2j\le n$ and $\binom{n}{j}=t$, so $2^j \le t$ by
Proposition~\ref{prop:growth}.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:fibre}
For $t\ge2$ and $b\ge2$, at most two occurrences of $t$ have folded index $b$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Such an occurrence satisfies $\binom{n}{b}=t$; column uniqueness
(Corollary~\ref{cor:coluniq}) forces a single row $n_0$, and within it the position is
$(n_0,b)$ or $(n_0,n_0-b)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Logarithmic bound]\label{thm:log}
For all $t \ge 2$, $\ N(t)\le 2\log_2 t$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Split $\Occ(t)$ by whether $\fold\le1$. The first part has at most $2$ elements
(Lemma~\ref{lem:bdry}). The second maps under $\fold$ into the integer interval
$[2,\lfloor\log_2 t\rfloor]$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:foldlog}), of cardinality
$\lfloor\log_2 t\rfloor-1$, and each fibre has at most $2$ elements
(Lemma~\ref{lem:fibre}). Hence $N(t)\le 2+2(\lfloor\log_2 t\rfloor-1)=2\lfloor\log_2 t\rfloor$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The best bound in the literature is $O(\log t/\log\log t)$. The improvement uses in addition
that the rows $n_1>n_2>\cdots$ attached to distinct columns $k_1<k_2<\cdots$ satisfy
$n_i \ge 2k_i$ \emph{simultaneously}, so the estimate $t=\binom{n_i}{k_i}\ge(n_i/k_i)^{k_i}$
may be applied to the largest column $k_r \ge r+1$ rather than to each column separately.
Theorem~\ref{thm:log} discards everything except ``one row per column''.
\end{remark}

\subsection{The row reformulation}

Let $R(t):=\{n : \exists k,\ \binom{n}{k}=t\}$.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:rows}
For $t\ge2$, $\ N(t)\le 2\lvert R(t)\rvert$.
\end{theorem}

Since also $\lvert R(t)\rvert \le N(t)$, Singmaster's conjecture is \emph{equivalent} to a
bound on $\lvert R(t)\rvert$: the column bookkeeping disappears and the problem becomes
``how many $n$ can satisfy $\binom{n}{k_n}=t$ for some $k_n$?''

\section{Small values and primes}\label{sec:small}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:small}
$N(2)=1$, $N(3)=N(4)=N(5)=2$, $N(6)=3$, $N(10)=4$.
\end{theorem}

Explicitly $6=\binom{6}{1}=\binom{6}{5}=\binom{4}{2}$ and
$10=\binom{10}{1}=\binom{10}{9}=\binom{5}{2}=\binom{5}{3}$; each evaluation is a finite check
inside the exhaustive box of Proposition~\ref{prop:loc}.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:two}
For every $t\ge3$, $N(t)\ge2$, witnessed by $\binom{t}{1}$ and $\binom{t}{t-1}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Primes]\label{thm:prime}
If $p$ is an odd prime then $N(p)=2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $\binom{n}{k}=p$ then $\binom{n}{k}k!\,(n-k)!=n!$ gives $p \mid n!$, so $p \le n$; with
$n \le p$ this yields $n=p$. In row $p$, an entry with $2 \le k \le p-2$ satisfies
$\binom{p}{2}=p(p-1)/2\le\binom{p}{k}$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:choose2}), and $p(p-1)/2>p$ for
$p \ge 5$; for $p=3$ no such $k$ exists.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:one}
For $t \ge 2$: $N(t)=1$ if and only if $t=2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:four}
For every $n\ge5$, $N\!\left(\binom{n}{2}\right)\ge4$. In particular $\{t : N(t)\ge4\}$ is
infinite.
\end{theorem}

\section{Why sixes are abundant: a Fibonacci family}\label{sec:fib}

A value $t=\binom{n}{k}$ with $2\le k\le n-2$ occupies at least four positions:
$(n,k)$, $(n,n-k)$, $(t,1)$, $(t,t-1)$. Two extra positions appear precisely when
\begin{equation}\label{eq:cross}
\binom{n}{k}=\binom{n-1}{k+1}.
\end{equation}

\begin{lemma}[Cross-row identity]\label{lem:cross}
Equation~\eqref{eq:cross} holds if and only if $n(k+1)=(n-k)(n-k-1)$.
\end{lemma}

With $m=n-k$ this is equivalent to $(5m-1)(m-1)$ being a perfect square: a Pell-type
condition, whose classical solutions come from Fibonacci numbers.

\begin{lemma}[Cassini at odd index]\label{lem:cassini}
For all $i \ge 0$, $F_{2i+3}^{\,2}=F_{2i+2}F_{2i+4}+1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Fibonacci family]\label{thm:fib}
Set $n_i := F_{2i+4}F_{2i+5}$ and $k_i := F_{2i+2}F_{2i+5}$. Then $n_i(k_i+1)=(n_i-k_i)(n_i-k_i-1)$,
hence $\binom{n_i}{k_i}=\binom{n_i-1}{k_i+1}$.
\end{theorem}

For $i=0$: $n_0=3\cdot5=15$, $k_0=1\cdot5=5$, giving $\binom{15}{5}=\binom{14}{6}=3003$.
For $i=1$: $n_1=8\cdot13=104$, $k_1=3\cdot13=39$, giving $\binom{104}{39}=\binom{103}{40}$.

\begin{theorem}[Infinitely many sixes]\label{thm:six}
For every $i \ge 0$, $N\!\left(\binom{n_i}{k_i}\right)\ge6$, the six positions being
$(n_i,k_i)$, $(n_i,n_i-k_i)$, $(n_i-1,k_i+1)$, $(n_i-1,n_i-k_i-2)$, $(t,1)$, $(t,t-1)$ where
$t=\binom{n_i}{k_i}$. Since $t\to\infty$, for every $M$ there is $t>M$ with $N(t)\ge6$.
\end{theorem}

The value $3003$ is exceptional because it is simultaneously a member of the cross-row family
(six positions) and a triangular number, $3003=\binom{78}{2}=\binom{78}{76}$ (two more).

\section{The parity criterion}\label{sec:parity}

Partition $\Occ(t)$ by the position of $k$ relative to the centre of its row:
\[
L(t):=\{(n,k)\in\Occ(t): 2k<n\},\quad
R'(t):=\{(n,k)\in\Occ(t): n<2k\},\quad
Z(t):=\{(n,k)\in\Occ(t): n=2k\}.
\]

\begin{lemma}[Reflection]\label{lem:refl}
For $t\ge2$ the map $(n,k)\mapsto(n,n-k)$ is a bijection $L(t)\to R'(t)$. Hence
\begin{equation}\label{eq:refl}
N(t)=2\lvert L(t)\rvert+\lvert Z(t)\rvert.
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:centeruniq}
$\lvert Z(t)\rvert\le1$ for all $t\ge2$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Elements of $Z(t)$ have the form $(2m,m)$ with $\binom{2m}{m}=t$, and
$m\mapsto\binom{2m}{m}$ is strictly increasing since
$\binom{2m+2}{m+1}=2\left(\binom{2m}{m}+\binom{2m}{m+1}\right)>\binom{2m}{m}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Parity criterion]\label{thm:parity}
For $t \ge 2$, $N(t)$ is odd if and only if $t=\binom{2m}{m}$ for some $m \ge 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By~\eqref{eq:refl}, $N(t)\equiv\lvert Z(t)\rvert \pmod 2$, and $\lvert Z(t)\rvert\in\{0,1\}$
is $1$ exactly when $t$ is a central binomial coefficient.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:57}
If $N(t)\in\{5,7\}$ then $t$ is a central binomial coefficient, i.e.\ $t$ belongs to
$2,6,20,70,252,924,3432,12870,48620,184756,705432,\dots$
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:cond}
If $N\!\left(\binom{2m}{m}\right)=3$ for every $m \ge 2$, then no integer has multiplicity
exactly $5$ or exactly $7$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:three}
For $m \ge 2$, $N\!\left(\binom{2m}{m}\right)\ge3$.
\end{proposition}

\section{Making the reduction effective}\label{sec:eff}

\begin{theorem}[Sandwich theorem]\label{thm:sandwich}
Let $m \ge 1$. If $k \le n \le 2m$ and $(n,k)\ne(2m,m)$, then
$\binom{n}{k}<\binom{2m}{m}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $j=\fold(n,k)$, so $\binom{n}{j}=\binom{n}{k}$ and $2j\le n$. If $j=0$ then
$\binom{n}{k}=1<2\le\binom{2m}{m}$. Otherwise $j \ge 1$ and $j<m$: if $n<2m$ then
$2j\le n<2m$; if $n=2m$ then $k \ne m$, so $j=\min(k,2m-k)<m$. Now
$\binom{n}{j}\le\binom{2m}{j}<\binom{2m}{m}$ by Proposition~\ref{prop:mono} and
Proposition~\ref{prop:uni}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Effective criterion, quadratic window]\label{thm:eff2}
Let $m \ge 2$, $t=\binom{2m}{m}$, and let $N \ge 2$ satisfy $t<\binom{N}{2}$. If
$\binom{n}{k}\ne t$ for all $2m<n<N$ and $2\le k\le\lfloor n/2\rfloor$, then $N(t)=3$.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Computational form of the equality test}

For large $n$ the predicate $\binom{n}{k}=t$ is expensive by Pascal recursion. We use
\[
\binom{n}{k}=t \iff n^{\underline{k}} = k!\,t,\qquad
n^{\underline{k}} := n(n-1)\cdots(n-k+1),
\]
valid since $n^{\underline{k}}=k!\binom{n}{k}$: the descending factorial costs $k$
multiplications rather than $\Theta(nk)$ additions.

\subsection{Two structural reductions}

\begin{theorem}[Column collapse]\label{thm:collapse}
Let $m \ge 1$, $2 \le k$, $2k \le n$, $n>2m$ and $\binom{n}{k}=\binom{2m}{m}$. Then $k<m$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $k \ge m$ then $\binom{2m}{m}\le\binom{2k}{k}\le\binom{n}{k}=\binom{2m}{m}$, so all are
equalities; but $k>m$ makes the first strict, and $k=m$ makes the second strict (as
$2k=2m<n$).
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Triangular obstruction]\label{thm:tri}
$\binom{n}{2}=t$ has a solution $n$ if and only if $8t+1$ is a perfect square.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\binom{n}{2}=t \iff n^2-n-2t=0 \iff (2n-1)^2=8t+1$.
\end{proof}

A single integer square-root test therefore eliminates the entire column $k=2$; afterwards
every surviving interior occurrence has $k \ge 3$, hence $\binom{n}{3}\le t$, and the row
window shrinks from $N\approx\sqrt{2t}$ to $N\approx(6t)^{1/3}$. For $m=20$
($t=\binom{40}{20}=137{,}846{,}528{,}820$) the box shrinks from roughly
$524{,}248\times262{,}124$ to $9{,}347\times17$.

\begin{theorem}[Effective criterion, cubic window]\label{thm:eff3}
Let $m \ge 3$ and $t=\binom{2m}{m}$. Suppose $8t+1$ is not a perfect square, and let $N$
satisfy $t<\binom{N}{3}$. If no pair $(n,k)$ with $2m<n<N$ and $3 \le k<m$ satisfies
$\binom{n}{k}=t$, then $N(t)=3$.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Results of the searches}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:m20}
$N\!\left(\binom{2m}{m}\right)=3$ for every $2 \le m \le 20$; explicitly each of
\[
6,\ 20,\ 70,\ 252,\ 924,\ 3432,\ 12870,\ 48620,\ 184756,\ 705432,\ 2704156,\ 10400600,
\]
\[
40116600,\ 155117520,\ 601080390,\ 2333606220,\ 9075135300,\ 35345263800,\ 137846528820
\]
occurs exactly three times in Pascal's triangle.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Unconditional exclusion of $5$ and $7$]\label{thm:no57}
No $t$ with $2 \le t<538{,}257{,}874{,}440$ satisfies $N(t)\in\{5,7\}$. Moreover every odd
value of $N(t)$ in that range equals $1$ or $3$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The bound is $\binom{42}{21}$, the next central binomial coefficient after $\binom{40}{20}$.
If $t<\binom{42}{21}$ has odd multiplicity, then $t=\binom{2m}{m}$ with $m \le 20$ by
Theorem~\ref{thm:parity}; $m=1$ gives $N(2)=1$, and $2 \le m \le 20$ gives $N(t)=3$ by
Theorem~\ref{thm:m20}.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:no57} is a statement about \emph{all} integers below half a trillion,
obtained from nineteen finite searches over a structurally distinguished sequence rather than
a scan of half a trillion values.

\section{Exact multiplicities and the maximum below $10^6$}\label{sec:exact}

\begin{theorem}[Exact algorithm]\label{thm:algo}
Let $t \ge 3$ and let $N \ge 2$ satisfy $t<\binom{N}{2}$. Then
\[
N(t)=2+\#\left\{(n,k) : n,k<N,\ 2\le k\le n-2,\ \binom{n}{k}=t\right\}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The boundary occurrences $(t,1)$, $(t,t-1)$ are always present and distinct for $t\ge3$; any
other is interior, so $\binom{n}{2}\le t<\binom{N}{2}$ gives $n<N$, and $k \le n<N$.
\end{proof}

The box has side $N=O(\sqrt t)$, so the procedure performs $O(t)$ equality tests, each of cost
$O(k)$ multiplications.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:3003}
$N(3003)=8$, the eight positions being
$\binom{3003}{1}$, $\binom{3003}{3002}$, $\binom{78}{2}$, $\binom{78}{76}$,
$\binom{15}{5}$, $\binom{15}{10}$, $\binom{14}{6}$, $\binom{14}{8}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:sixes}
$N(120)=N(210)=N(1540)=N(7140)=N(11628)=N(24310)=6$.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{The maximum below one million}

For $t \ge 3$ exactly one left occurrence is the trivial $(t,1)$, so with $L^{\mathrm{int}}(t)$
denoting the left occurrences with column $k \ge 2$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:refined}
N(t)=2+2\lvert L^{\mathrm{int}}(t)\rvert+\lvert Z(t)\rvert.
\end{equation}
Column uniqueness means a left interior occurrence's column determines its row. Hence at most
one has $k=2$; and if $t<10^6$, one with $k \ge 3$ satisfies $2^k \le t$, so $k<20$, and
$\binom{n}{2}\le t$, so $n<1415$ (since $\binom{1415}{2}=1{,}000{,}405>10^6$). All such lie in
an explicit $1415\times20$ box.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:search}
No value below $10^6$ occurs twice with column index $\ge3$, except
$3003=\binom{15}{5}=\binom{14}{6}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:le6}
For $2 \le t<10^6$ with $t \ne 3003$, $N(t)\le6$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:search}, $\lvert L^{\mathrm{int}}(t)\rvert\le2$, so \eqref{eq:refined} gives
$N(t)\le 2+4+1=7$; multiplicity $7$ is excluded by Theorem~\ref{thm:no57}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Maximum below $10^6$]\label{thm:max}
For $2 \le t<10^6$ we have $N(t)\le8$, with equality if and only if $t=3003$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Discussion}\label{sec:disc}

\subsection{Where the difficulty lives}

For the parity question, the difficulty is entirely concentrated on the sequence
$\binom{2m}{m}$: by Corollary~\ref{cor:cond}, multiplicities $5$ and $7$ never occur if and
only if every central binomial coefficient has multiplicity exactly $3$. Any counterexample
would be a coincidence $\binom{n}{k}=\binom{2m}{m}$ with $n>2m$ and $3 \le k<m$, in an
explicitly bounded and (after Theorems~\ref{thm:collapse} and~\ref{thm:tri}) small region.

For Singmaster's conjecture itself, Theorem~\ref{thm:rows} says the problem is about counting
\emph{rows}. Known upper bounds proceed by bounding columns; a row-side argument would be
genuinely new.

\subsection{Three levels of evidence}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{Unconditional general theorems}: the logarithmic bound, the prime evaluation, the
parity criterion, the sandwich theorem.
\item \emph{Infinite families}: the Fibonacci construction giving infinitely many sixes and the
triangular construction giving infinitely many fours.
\item \emph{Certified finite ranges}: $N(\binom{2m}{m})=3$ for $m\le20$, hence no fives or
sevens below $\binom{42}{21}$; the exact values $N(3003)=8$ etc.; the maximality of $3003$
below $10^6$.
\end{enumerate}

The interplay between levels 1 and 3 is the methodological point: a structural theorem
(parity) turns an infeasible verification into nineteen feasible ones.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Bold]
There is an absolute constant $c$ with $N(t)\le c\,\log t/\log\log t$ for $t \ge 16$.
\end{conjecture}
The unconditional result available here is $N(t)\le2\log_2 t$. The key insight is that the
present proof discards everything except ``one row per column''; the sharper estimate uses that
the rows attached to distinct columns satisfy $n_i \ge 2k_i$ simultaneously, so
$t=\binom{n_i}{k_i}\ge(n_i/k_i)^{k_i}$ may be applied to the largest column $k_r \ge r+1$. Only
the analytic estimate $(n/k)^k\le\binom{n}{k}$ remains to be added.

\begin{conjecture}[Grand challenge]
$\exists C\ \forall t\ge2:\ N(t)\le C$, and in fact $C=8$, attained only at $t=3003$.
\end{conjecture}
The key insight is the reduction $N(t)\le2\lvert R(t)\rvert$: since a row carries a given value
at most twice, Singmaster's conjecture is equivalent to a bound on the number of rows meeting
$t$. Every partial result about $\lvert R(t)\rvert$ transfers immediately to $N(t)$.

\begin{conjecture}[Bold, cross-domain]
Every solution of $\binom{n}{k}=\binom{n-1}{k+1}$ with $1 \le k \le n-2$ is a member of the
Fibonacci family, i.e.\ $n=F_{2i+4}F_{2i+5}$, $k=F_{2i+2}F_{2i+5}$ for some $i \ge 0$.
Equivalently, with $m=n-k$, the integer $(5m-1)(m-1)$ is a perfect square if and only if
$m=F_{2i+3}F_{2i+5}$.
\end{conjecture}

Further natural targets: extend Theorem~\ref{thm:m20} to $m \le 40$ by eliminating the columns
$k=3,4$ with congruence or square-testing obstructions analogous to Theorem~\ref{thm:tri};
decide whether $\{t : N(t)\ge8\}$ is infinite or equal to $\{3003\}$; establish the exact
maximum of $N$ below $10^9$; and investigate analogues of the parity criterion for other
reflection-symmetric arrays such as Gaussian binomial coefficients and Stirling triangles.

\section{Conclusion}

Pascal's triangle contains each of its entries astonishingly few times, and Singmaster's
question of whether ``few'' means ``boundedly few'' remains open. Nevertheless a great deal is
provable by elementary means: the multiplicity function is finite and computable; it is at most
$2\log_2 t$; it equals $2$ for all odd primes; it is odd precisely at central binomial
coefficients; it is at least $6$ infinitely often through a Fibonacci mechanism; it equals $3$
at the first nineteen non-trivial central binomial coefficients, hence never equals $5$ or $7$
below half a trillion; and below one million it attains its maximum, $8$, uniquely at $3003$.

The parity criterion is the conceptual pivot: it explains why odd multiplicities are exotic and
converts the search for fives and sevens into a question about a density-zero sequence.

